# SSH Hardening

## Objective

Improve the security of remote access to my Ubuntu Server by using SSH key authentication instead of password authentication.

## Configuration

### SSH Key Authentication

Generated an ED25519 SSH key pair on my Windows PC.

The private key is stored securely on the Windows client and is not stored in this repository.

The public key was added to the Ubuntu Server:

```text
~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

### File Permissions

Configured SSH directory permissions:

```bash
chmod 700 ~/.ssh
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

### Password Authentication

Disabled SSH password authentication in:

```text
/etc/ssh/sshd_config
```

Configuration:

```text
PasswordAuthentication no
PubkeyAuthentication yes
```

### Configuration Testing

Tested the SSH configuration before restarting the service:

```bash
sudo sshd -t
```

The configuration passed without errors.

### Testing

Successfully connected from my Windows PC using SSH key authentication.

The SSH key is protected with a passphrase.

## Security Improvement

Password-based SSH authentication was disabled, reducing the risk of attackers gaining access through password guessing or brute-force attempts.

